>follow proceedure. Mysticism makes no promises.

On the contrary, it makes plenty.  Doesn't follow through on them so well
(which I guess is what you meant).

>If you haven't levitated
>yet, you just haven't done it long enough. If the spirits haven't
>visited you yet, well maybe they won't. God works in mysterious ways
>that you shouldn't try to understand. If gluing a healing crystal to your
>forehead hasn't cured your cancer yet, maybe your not resonting
>with the cosmos.

Well, there is mysticism and mysticism.  You hold up flying yogis,
channelling, and crystal healing to scorn (rightly, IMHO); nevertheless
other areas which might be classified as "mysticism" are, I believe, more
reputable.  I am thinking of Buddhist meditative techniques, Sufism,
General Semantics, and similar things.  Or, for that matter, just chilling
out with nature in the wilderness.  In short, techniques for altering one's
mind other than verbal argument.  This is something with which those fields
of study which the word "science" usually covers have had little success
with as yet.

Whether you call any of these these "mysticism", "psychology", or "science"
is not important.  All that matters is what works.  These things are
unlikely to provide cures for cancer, easy travel to distant planets, or
greatly prolonged lifespan, but that is not their purpose, any more than
cold fusion will enhance anyone's mental functioning.

