You might call them the Five Principles of What-Passes-For-Rational Discussion.
They are pervasive everywhere, not just on the Extropians List.  It's amazing
how people who act as though they worship at the altar of Rationality become so
irrational when faced with differing values and purposes.

grigsby: <I believe that drooling over nonexistent technology will get us
nowhere, and that we must actively seek ways of life that will allow us the time
and tools to explore and invent new technology.>

The Curse of Utopianism is unbearably heavy:  The Eternal Recurrence of Wishful
Thinking.  This is your strongest argument.  I am largely in agreement that
while we can speculate about things 60 years in the future as a means of
envisioning goals for purposeful action, it is far more important to deal with
shaping social values over the next 3-10 years.

grigsby: <I believe that the challenge of boundless expansion requires that we
know our current limitations so as to effectively transcend them.>

Sort of like:  "Know thyself"?  I think that to "know" limitations means to
"know" from both sides of the limitation.  It is impossible to "know"
limitations without constantly pushing onward and outward in every direction to
find out what is achievable now versus achievable with more work.

grigsby: <Current human behavior (exponential population growth driving
ever-increasing demands on the Earth's resources) is not sustainable.>

