From: Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Jan 94 14:37:41 EST
Subject: [#94-1-650] LEGAL:  Proposal to encourage PPL participation

In #94-1-647 Mike Price writes:
>How long have you been on the List?

At least since 1991.  That's as far back as my mail logs go.

>A PPL system, of sorts, has been available for under a _month_.

If you mean that more than one PPL have coexisted for only a month, I 
agree.  But I believe that ExI claims that PPL have "officially" existed 
since the beginning.  When I tried to declare myself PPL-free, I was told 
by Harry (PPL-1 administrator at that time) that the PPL system had always 
existed and was always intended by ExI, and that historically outlaws have 
always been removed from the List.

>A poly-centric system for law requires a market mechanism for the
>interaction of PPLs.  The guidelines for the interaction of PPLs have
>not been expressed or codified.  This is what I'm trying to put into
>place with my interPPL charter proposal.  I want to see a framework to
>exist for the PPLs to exist and interact with each other in.  Until then
>we do not have a PPL system for list justice.

I believe you are differentiating the current "PPL system" and a "true PPL" 
system.  I see your points and agree with the differences.  In many very 
real aspects, what we have had here on the list is not a true PPL system.

>No, the test is what proportion of the _active_ posters are in different
>PPLs.  Lurkers are not going to be very bothered by which PPL they're
>in.  A large number of active posters have moved from PPL1, including
>you, I think....  


Very good point I had not considered!  Indeed, only active participants 
will have an influence in the PPL experiment.  Thus, counting only active 
posters, we may already have a good distribution between the two existing 
PPLs.  Perhaps no action to force dispersal to other PPLs is necessary.

Personally, I would still like to see more PPLs form, however.  I also 
would like some way to differentiate those who choose PPL-1 as their 
personal standards of law, and those that just don't care.  (I also would 
prefer a scheme for letting people choose a new PPL when theirs goes 
defunct, instead of moving them back into a PPL that they chose to leave.)

It will be helpful if people keep in mind that this is an experiment, and 
sometimes unforseen situations are encountered.  When this happens, people 
want to discuss the ramifications of the system and perhaps implement 
needed changes.  Requesting that people stop discussing this topic is like 
requesting that we participate in an experiment without discussing the 
results.  Enforcing existing policies when new or unusual circumstances 
occur is like saying that the experiment is over and the test subject has 
already been proved to be viable.  In reality, we are still experimenting 
with the system.  Experimentation should be interesting and fun.

>A large number of active posters have moved from PPL1, including
>you, I think....  

Yes, I have moved out of PPL-1.  I first tried to leave it without joining 
another, but ExI policy forbids this.  I then tried to form my own PPL, but 
did not have time to submit all of the requisite documentation.  I joined 
Tim May's PPL as soon as it formed, and was its first (nonfounding) member.  
When Tim left, I requested a transfer to Anarchy-PPL.  I was already 
discussing such a move before Tim left.  I like Anarchy-PPL, and no longer 
am (currently) working on forming my own.
