From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Fri, 28 Jan 94 15:18:57 PST
Subject: [#94-1-656] [#94-1-498] Pancritical Rationalism

I asked:
>This "Pancritical Rationalism" has been getting a lot of press here
>lately.  Could anyone consisely yet precisely summarize this point of
>view, in say 500 words or less?  Or is this yet another "philosophy"
>which can only be understood by reading three volumes of the great
>master, ten volumes of commentary by learned students, and only then
>with a full appreciation of the historical context of other views this
>master was reacting to.  (If the latter, I had enough of that in
>academic philosophy, thank you.)

Dean Tribble responded with a "A brief summary of PCR", Steve Withman
added some comments, and Tim Starr has engaged them in discussion.

While concise, Dean's summary fails to be precise, at least to me. I
feel like a linguist trying to learn the language of a new tribe --
these folks are obviously not using words the way I do, so I need to
carefully see how these words are used in practice, and to find a
translation to my language that makes them seem like reasonable folks.
Except with philosophical tribes, they then complain, "no that's not
what we mean at all".

For example, Dean says: "beliefs are never true or false, just more or
less credible".  Now what I mean by "true" is something like: "`snow
is white' is true" if and only if "snow is white".  So when Dean
says "act on the basis of the beliefs with the most credibility", I'm
tempted to translate "Credible = Most likely to be true", but then
Dean would object.

Dean says:
>Thus, statements that don't make a prediction (or cannot be cast so as
>to make a prediction) have little rational content

Now here I'd translate "Has rational content = Has what I like".  Dean
mainly likes to make predictions.

Dean says:
>things don't need to be justified (in terms of axioms) to be believed.  

Now how do I translate "justified"?  Sounds like he means "implied by
axioms".  But since folks believe contradictory things, which cannot be
axiomitized, then of course they can believe things which can't be
axiomitized.  And any non-contradictory set of beliefs can be
axiomitized (axioms found which imply them all), so this doesn't
distinguish much.  I guess I'll translate Dean as saying that he
doesn't see much value in axiomitizing beliefs.

I could go on playing linguist, but I'm not sure what the point is.
Like Tim, I don't see what the payoff is from learning to speak this
language.  I do see value in sometimes axiomitizing beliefs, finding a
smaller set that implies them all, and in doing things besides
prediction.

Robin Hanson

