From: "Phil G. Fraering" <pgf@srl01.cacs.usl.edu>
Date: Fri, 28 Jan 1994 18:26:44 -0600
Subject: [#94-1-660] Note on Brazilian Resource Extraction

I'm operating on exclude all and including a couple "bellweather" posters,
so please excuse me if someone else has covered this already.

Brazil has come up a great deal in this discussion about resources,
economics, and ecology. I'd like to point out a couple things I feel
fairly certian about.

Brazilian exploitation of the rain forest areas, both to expand its
agricultural base, and to exploit the mineral resources there, do not
seem to be the result of any great market pressures. Brazil apparently
engaged, during the time period from the sixties to today, in the use of
old mercantilistic economic policies with regard to Amazonia: they literally
woke up one day and realized that it was nearly undeveloped and thought that
that would in turn render invalid their claim to political sovereignity
over the region. In order to "consolidate" their hold, they started a
number of programs to exploit the region. It doesn't matter to them that
after they've burned down the forest, put up some cattle (that won't
find very fertile grazing for more than a couple years) or put in a farm
that won't last more than a couple years, or that some of the mineral
resources they've used are of questionable profitability... because as
long as they've ruined it, Peru/Columbia/Venezuela/Whoever can't have
it, and that's good enough for them.

It has been alleged by the Venezuelan government that the Brazilian government
has been funding illegal "wildcat" mining enterprises on Venezuela's side of
the border, that are apparently terrorizing both indian tribes and whoever
Venezuela can get in to work legitimately to the point where Venezuela is
having problems attracting honest people to the area.

In short, the destruction of Amazonia's ecology seems to have more to
do with old-style nationalism than any-style capitalism.

I'm sorry if I've said all this before and am simply repeating myself; I
thought, however, that this does have some bearing on the current "debate."

Phil Fraering

